 "scripts": {
  "lesson": [
   {
    "id": "L01",
    "seg": "Open",
    "shot": "S01",
    "text": "Two flat sheets of cells. One week later, three. How does that happen, and where does each new layer come from?",
    "facts": [],
    "hold": 2.0,
    "kind": "question",
    "_start": 0.0,
    "_words": 21,
    "_dur": 8.8,
    "refs": []
   },
   {
    "id": "L02",
    "seg": "Open",
    "shot": "S02",
    "text": "Three terms first: germ layers, primitive node, notochord.",
    "facts": [],
    "new": [
     "germ layers",
     "primitive node",
     "notochord"
    ],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "kind": "terms",
    "_start": 10.8,
    "_words": 8,
    "_dur": 3.4,
    "refs": []
   },
   {
    "id": "L03",
    "seg": "1 The groove",
    "shot": "S03",
    "text": "This is the disc at the very start of week three. Its upper layer, from week two, is the epiblast.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.04#f1"
    ],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "_start": 15.2,
    "_words": 20,
    "_dur": 8.4,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.04#f1",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E04.01",
      "cite": "E04.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L04",
    "seg": "1 The groove",
    "shot": "S03",
    "text": "In week three the disc makes three germ layers: ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm. That process is gastrulation.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.01#f1"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 24.6,
    "_words": 17,
    "_dur": 7.1,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.01#f1",
      "mark": "✔✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01 · R05b"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L05",
    "seg": "1 The groove",
    "shot": "S04",
    "text": "It starts when the primitive streak appears on the epiblast, around days fourteen to fifteen.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.01#f2"
    ],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "_start": 33.2,
    "_words": 15,
    "_dur": 6.3,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.01#f2",
      "mark": "✔✔",
      "section": "E05.01, E06.06 Tbl 6.4",
      "cite": "E05.01, E06.06 Tbl 6.4 · R06 CS6"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L06",
    "seg": "1 The groove",
    "shot": "S04",
    "text": "The streak first shows as a faint line in the caudal midline. By days fifteen to sixteen it is a narrow groove with raised edges.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.02#f1"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 40.5,
    "_words": 25,
    "_dur": 10.5,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.02#f1",
      "mark": "✔✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01 · R06 CS6"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L07",
    "seg": "1 The groove",
    "shot": "S05",
    "text": "Its cranial end is a low mound, with a small pit at its centre. Keep an eye on it.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.02#f2"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 52.5,
    "_words": 19,
    "_dur": 8.0,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.02#f2",
      "mark": "✔✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01 · R06 CS8"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L08",
    "seg": "1 The groove",
    "shot": "S06",
    "text": "The streak also marks the body's long axis. It lies at the tail end; the head end is the opposite end.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.02#f3"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 61.9,
    "_words": 21,
    "_dur": 8.8,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.02#f3",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01, E05.03",
      "cite": "E05.01, E05.03"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L09",
    "seg": "2 The dive",
    "shot": "S07",
    "text": "Back to day fifteen. Let's follow one epiblast cell. We have marked it.",
    "facts": [],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "kind": "frame",
    "_start": 72.3,
    "_words": 13,
    "_dur": 5.5,
    "refs": []
   },
   {
    "id": "L10",
    "seg": "2 The dive",
    "shot": "S08",
    "text": "Epiblast cells move toward the streak, change shape, let go of their neighbours and slip underneath. This inward move is called invagination.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.03#f1"
    ],
    "new": [
     "invagination"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 78.7,
    "_words": 22,
    "_dur": 9.2,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.03#f1",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L11",
    "seg": "2 The dive",
    "shot": "S09",
    "text": "The first cells in push into the hypoblast and replace it, forming the embryo's endoderm.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.03#f2"
    ],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "_start": 89.4,
    "_words": 15,
    "_dur": 6.3,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.03#f2",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L12",
    "seg": "2 The dive",
    "shot": "S09",
    "text": "So the hypoblast does not turn into endoderm. Epiblast cells take its place.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.01#m1"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 96.7,
    "_words": 13,
    "_dur": 5.5,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.01#m1",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L13",
    "seg": "2 The dive",
    "shot": "S10",
    "text": "Our cell goes in later. It settles between the epiblast and the new endoderm, as mesoderm.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.03#f3"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 103.7,
    "_words": 16,
    "_dur": 6.7,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.03#f3",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L14",
    "seg": "2 The dive",
    "shot": "S11",
    "text": "Mesoderm spreads sideways and toward the head.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.03#f4"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 111.9,
    "_words": 7,
    "_dur": 2.9,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.03#f4",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L15",
    "seg": "2 The dive",
    "shot": "S12",
    "text": "Cells that stay in the epiblast become ectoderm.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.03#f5"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 116.3,
    "_words": 8,
    "_dur": 3.4,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.03#f5",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L16",
    "seg": "2 The dive",
    "shot": "S13",
    "text": "Cut across the disc. Ectoderm is the top layer. Mesoderm is the middle layer. Endoderm is the bottom layer, facing the yolk sac.",
    "facts": [
     "E00.01.04#f2",
     "E00.01.04#f3",
     "E00.01.04#f4"
    ],
    "new": [
     "yolk sac"
    ],
    "hold": 2.0,
    "_start": 121.2,
    "_words": 23,
    "_dur": 9.7,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E00.01.04#f2",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     },
     {
      "ref": "E00.01.04#f3",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     },
     {
      "ref": "E00.01.04#f4",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L17",
    "seg": "2 The dive",
    "shot": "S13",
    "text": "So all three germ layers come from the epiblast. The epiblast is not the ectoderm; ectoderm is only the part it leaves behind.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.04#f4",
     "E05.01.01#m3"
    ],
    "hold": 2.0,
    "_start": 132.8,
    "_words": 23,
    "_dur": 9.7,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.04#f4",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01, E05.07",
      "cite": "E05.01, E05.07"
     },
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.01#m3",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L18",
    "seg": "3 The rod",
    "shot": "S14",
    "text": "Our cell is out in the mesoderm now. Back to day sixteen, at that mound: the primitive node.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.02#f2"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 144.5,
    "_words": 18,
    "_dur": 7.6,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.02#f2",
      "mark": "✔✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01 · R06 CS8"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L18b",
    "seg": "3 The rod",
    "shot": "S14",
    "text": "One stream of cells does something different there. Cells entering at the node move straight forward in the midline, toward the head end.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.02.01#f1"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 153.6,
    "_words": 23,
    "_dur": 9.7,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.02.01#f1",
      "mark": "✔✔",
      "section": "E05.02",
      "cite": "E05.02 · R05a, R06 CS7"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L19",
    "seg": "3 The rod",
    "shot": "S15",
    "text": "For a short time they sit within the endoderm, as a flat plate.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.02.01#f2"
    ],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "_start": 164.7,
    "_words": 13,
    "_dur": 5.5,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.02.01#f2",
      "mark": "✔✔",
      "section": "E05.02",
      "cite": "E05.02 · R05a"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L20",
    "seg": "3 The rod",
    "shot": "S15",
    "text": "Then the plate lifts off the endoderm and rolls into a solid rod: the notochord.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.02.01#f3"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 171.2,
    "_words": 15,
    "_dur": 6.3,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.02.01#f3",
      "mark": "✔✔",
      "section": "E05.02",
      "cite": "E05.02 · R05a"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L21",
    "seg": "3 The rod",
    "shot": "S16",
    "text": "In the book, its head end forms first, by about day seventeen, and tail parts are added as the streak moves back.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.02.01#f4"
    ],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "_start": 179.0,
    "_words": 22,
    "_dur": 9.2,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.02.01#f4",
      "mark": "⚑",
      "section": "E05.02",
      "cite": "E05.02 · R05a"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L22",
    "seg": "3 The rod",
    "shot": "S16",
    "text": "Newer human data tell a different story. You'll find it on the notochord card.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.02.01#f6"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "kind": "flag-pointer",
    "_start": 189.2,
    "_words": 14,
    "_dur": 5.9,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.02.01#f6",
      "mark": "⚑",
      "section": "E05.02",
      "cite": "E05.02 · R05a"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L23",
    "seg": "3 The rod",
    "shot": "S17",
    "text": "The notochord lies in the midline and sends signals that help build the spine around it.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.02.01#f5"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 196.6,
    "_words": 16,
    "_dur": 6.7,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.02.01#f5",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.02, E05.07",
      "cite": "E05.02, E05.07"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L24",
    "seg": "3 The rod",
    "shot": "S18",
    "text": "In adults only bits of it remain, in the nucleus pulposus, at the core of each spinal disc, the cushion between the vertebrae. It does not become the vertebrae.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.02.01#f7",
     "E05.02.01#m1"
    ],
    "new": [
     "nucleus pulposus"
    ],
    "hold": 2.0,
    "_start": 204.8,
    "_words": 29,
    "_dur": 12.2,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.02.01#f7",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.02",
      "cite": "E05.02 · E10"
     },
     {
      "ref": "E05.02.01#m1",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.02",
      "cite": "E05.02 · E10"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L25",
    "seg": "3 The rod",
    "shot": "S19",
    "text": "And the mesoderm our cell joined goes on to build muscle, bone, connective tissue, the heart, vessels and blood.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.03#f6"
    ],
    "hold": 2.0,
    "_start": 218.9,
    "_words": 19,
    "_dur": 8.0,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.03#f6",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E06.02",
      "cite": "E06.02"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L26",
    "seg": "Clinical link",
    "shot": "S20",
    "text": "Clinical link. Here, at day seventeen, the groove our cell went through is still adding cells. It keeps going until the end of week four, then shrinks fast and disappears.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.02#f4"
    ],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "_start": 228.9,
    "_words": 30,
    "_dur": 12.6,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.02#f4",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.05",
      "cite": "E05.05"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L27",
    "seg": "Clinical link",
    "shot": "S21",
    "text": "Leftover streak cells near the base of the spine can form a sacrococcygeal teratoma, the most common tumour in newborns.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.02#f5",
     "E05.01.02#f6"
    ],
    "new": [
     "sacrococcygeal teratoma"
    ],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "_start": 242.5,
    "_words": 20,
    "_dur": 8.4,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.02#f5",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.05 CC",
      "cite": "E05.05 CC"
     },
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.02#f6",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.05 CC",
      "cite": "E05.05 CC"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L28",
    "seg": "Clinical link",
    "shot": "S21",
    "text": "It holds tissue from all three germ layers, and it is often found on scans before birth.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.02#f7"
    ],
    "hold": 1.5,
    "_start": 251.9,
    "_words": 17,
    "_dur": 7.1,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.02#f7",
      "mark": "✔✔",
      "section": "E05.05 CC",
      "cite": "E05.05 CC · R05g, R05h, R05i"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L29",
    "seg": "Close",
    "shot": "S22",
    "text": "So, back to our question. Cells went in through the streak. The first replaced the hypoblast as endoderm, the next became mesoderm, and those left on top became ectoderm.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.03#f2",
     "E05.01.03#f3",
     "E05.01.03#f5"
    ],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "_start": 260.5,
    "_words": 29,
    "_dur": 12.2,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.03#f2",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     },
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.03#f3",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     },
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.03#f5",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L30",
    "seg": "Close",
    "shot": "S22",
    "text": "Three layers, all from the epiblast.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.04#f4"
    ],
    "hold": 2.0,
    "_start": 273.7,
    "_words": 6,
    "_dur": 2.5,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.04#f4",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01, E05.07",
      "cite": "E05.01, E05.07"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L31",
    "seg": "Close",
    "shot": "S23",
    "text": "Two quick checks. On this section, tap the layer our cell joined.",
    "facts": [],
    "hold": 4.0,
    "kind": "check",
    "check": "spatial: tap Mesoderm on the day-17 cross-section (answer from E05.01.03#f3)",
    "_start": 278.2,
    "_words": 12,
    "_dur": 5.0,
    "refs": []
   },
   {
    "id": "L31a",
    "seg": "Close",
    "shot": "S23",
    "text": "The middle layer: mesoderm.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.03#f3"
    ],
    "hold": 2.0,
    "kind": "reveal",
    "_start": 287.2,
    "_words": 4,
    "_dur": 1.7,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.03#f3",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L32",
    "seg": "Close",
    "shot": "S23",
    "text": "And the hypoblast: does it become endoderm, or is it replaced?",
    "facts": [],
    "hold": 4.0,
    "kind": "check",
    "check": "E05.01.01 q2 (refutes m1), spoken choices; chips 'Becomes endoderm' / 'Is replaced'",
    "_start": 290.9,
    "_words": 11,
    "_dur": 4.6,
    "refs": []
   },
   {
    "id": "L32a",
    "seg": "Close",
    "shot": "S23",
    "text": "It is replaced. Epiblast cells take its place; it does not become endoderm.",
    "facts": [
     "E05.01.01#m1"
    ],
    "hold": 2.0,
    "kind": "reveal",
    "_start": 299.5,
    "_words": 13,
    "_dur": 5.5,
    "refs": [
     {
      "ref": "E05.01.01#m1",
      "mark": "✔",
      "section": "E05.01",
      "cite": "E05.01"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "L33",
    "seg": "Close",
    "shot": "S24",
    "text": "Next: how does this flat disc fold into a body?",
    "facts": [],
    "hold": 1.0,
    "kind": "teaser",
